# SOURCE-SHA256.json records the SHA-256 of each file's raw bytes, so a
# checkout that rewrites line endings produces a tree the manifest no longer
# describes. Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default -- including on
# GitHub's windows-latest runners -- which converted every text file to CRLF on
# checkout and made the release gate's manifest step fail on every commit while
# the Linux jobs passed.
#
# Normalize to LF in the repository and check out LF on every platform, so the
# bytes an operator hashes are the bytes that were signed. text=auto still lets
# Git detect and leave genuinely binary files alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files whose byte content is load-bearing: the
# manifest itself, the hash-locked build inputs, and the shell that runs on the
# air-gapped target. Never transform these, whatever Git infers.
SOURCE-SHA256.json -text
requirements-build.lock -text
*.sh -text

# Windows scripts keep exactly the bytes that were committed. run_gui.bat is
# stored with CRLF, and normalizing it would change its hash on every platform
# rather than only on Windows -- trading a Windows-only manifest failure for a
# universal one. cmd.exe is also the one interpreter here that can misparse an
# LF-only batch file, so leave these alone entirely.
*.bat -text
*.ps1 -text
